\subsection{What counts as passing?}

A grade is considered passing if it is either:
\begin{itemize}
\item [[complete]] (case-insensitive, so [[Complete]] or [[COMPLETE]] also work)
\item [[100]] (a numeric grade indicating full completion)
\end{itemize}

Anything else---including [[incomplete]], [[None]], or a missing
submission---counts as not passing.
Canvas can also expose the failing label [[must fix]].
Participation grading does not distinguish between different failing labels, so
we normalize that alias to [[F]] before checking whether the assignment passed.
<<helper functions>>=
def normalize_grade(grade):
  """Normalize Canvas failing aliases for participation grading."""
  if isinstance(grade, str) and grade.casefold() == "must fix":
    return "F"
  return grade
@

<<helper functions>>=
def is_passing_grade(grade):
  """
  Returns True if the grade indicates passing (complete or 100).
  """
  grade = normalize_grade(grade)
  if grade is None:
    return False
  if isinstance(grade, str):
    if grade.casefold() == "complete":
      return True
    if grade == "100":
      return True
  return False
@

\subsection{Summarizing a student's participation}

The [[summarize]] function iterates through all assignments and checks whether
each one has a passing grade.
We collect dates and graders along the way.
<<helper functions>>=
def summarize(user, assignments_list):
  """
  Extracts user's submissions for all participation assignments.
  Returns (grade, date, graders) where grade is P if all passed, F otherwise.
  """
  passed = []
  dates = []
  graders = []

  for assignment in assignments_list:
    <<get submission for assignment>>
    <<check if grade is passing>>
    <<add graders to [[graders]] list>>
    <<add date to [[dates]] list>>

  <<determine final grade>>

  return (final_grade, final_date, graders)
@

For each assignment, we attempt to fetch the student's submission.
If the submission doesn't exist (which can happen in rare cases), we treat it
as not passing.
<<get submission for assignment>>=
try:
  submission = assignment.get_submission(user,
                                        include=["submission_history"])
except ResourceDoesNotExist:
  passed.append(False)
  continue

submission.assignment = assignment
@

We extract the grade from the submission and check whether it's passing---unless
the assignment is a survey, which passes by being completed
(\cref{participation-surveys}).
<<check if grade is passing>>=
if is_survey(assignment):
  passed.append(survey_completed(submission))
else:
  passed.append(is_passing_grade(submission.grade))
@

We collect all graders from the submission history.
This ensures we credit everyone who participated in the grading process, not
just the last person to grade.
<<add graders to [[graders]] list>>=
graders += results.all_graders(submission)
@

For the date, we prefer the submission date but fall back to the grading date
if no submission was made (for example, oral presentations where the student
didn't submit anything).
<<add date to [[dates]] list>>=
grade_date = submission.submitted_at or submission.graded_at
if grade_date:
  grade_date = dt.date.fromisoformat(grade_date.split("T")[0])
  dates.append(grade_date)
@

\subsection{Determining the final grade}

The final grade is P if \emph{all} assignments passed, F otherwise.
If there are no dates (meaning the student has no activity at all), we return
[[None]] for both grade and date.
<<determine final grade>>=
if dates:
  final_date = max(dates)
  if all(passed):
    final_grade = "P"
  else:
    final_grade = "F"
else:
  final_date = None
  final_grade = None
@


\subsection{Surveys pass by completion}\label{participation-surveys}

The exception in [[<<check if grade is passing>>]] is the survey.
A survey's grade says nothing about the student (\cref{surveys}), and the
rule \enquote{[[complete]] or [[100]]} only fits it while the survey happens
to be worth 100 points: Canvas grades a submitted graded survey with its full
points, so a survey worth 50 would fail every student who answered it.
Completion is what participation asks for, so completion is what we check.
A missing submission needs no special case: it has already failed the
assignment, survey or not, in [[<<get submission for assignment>>]].

Let's verify on a group like the one above: a survey next to a
FeedbackFruits item graded out of 100 points.
A completed survey passes whatever its grade, the lowered [[50]] included,
while an uncompleted one fails the group.
<<test functions>>=
class TestSurveys:
    """Test that a survey passes by completion, not by its grade."""

    <<survey test methods>>
<<survey test methods>>=
def peer_review(self, grading_objects):
    return grading_objects.points(grading_objects.submission(
        "100", submitted_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z"))

def test_completed_survey_passes_whatever_its_grade(self, grading_objects):
    user = grading_objects.user()

    for survey_grade in ["100", "50", None]:
        survey = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(
            survey_grade, submitted_at="2026-01-11T10:00:00Z"))

        grade, grade_date, _ = summarize(
            user, [self.peer_review(grading_objects), survey])

        assert grade == "P"
        assert grade_date.isoformat() == "2026-01-11"

def test_uncompleted_survey_fails(self, grading_objects):
    user = grading_objects.user()
    dateless = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(None))
    absent = grading_objects.survey(None)

    for survey in [dateless, absent]:
        grade, _, _ = summarize(
            user, [self.peer_review(grading_objects), survey])

        assert grade == "F"
@

The completion rule is for surveys only.
A points-graded assignment that is not a survey still needs its [[100]]: a
FeedbackFruits item that the student submitted but that was graded [[50]] is
not passed.
<<survey test methods>>=
def test_points_assignment_still_needs_100(self, grading_objects):
    user = grading_objects.user()
    partial = grading_objects.points(grading_objects.submission(
        "50", submitted_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z"))

    grade, _, _ = summarize(user, [partial])

    assert grade == "F"
@


\subsection{Finding missing assignments}

For participation grading, a student is \enquote{missing} any assignment that
doesn't have a passing grade ([[complete]] or [[100]]).
Since this is conjunctive grading (all must pass), each incomplete assignment
prevents the student from passing the group.

Surveys are judged by completion here as well, so they go to
[[missing_surveys]] (\cref{surveys}) rather than to the grade check, which
would report a completed survey graded [[50]] as \enquote{not a passing
grade (50)}.
For everything else we already have [[is_passing_grade]] defined above, so we
simply reuse the shared implementation from [[canvaslms.cli.results]] with our
callback.
<<helper functions>>=
def missing_assignments(assignments_list, users_list,
                        optional_assignments=None):
  """
  Returns missing assignments for participation grading.

  Every uncompleted survey is missing. Any other assignment without
  'complete' or '100' is missing.
  """
  surveys, others = split_surveys(assignments_list)

  yield from missing_surveys(surveys, users_list, optional_assignments)
  yield from results.missing_assignments(
    others, users_list,
    optional_assignments=optional_assignments,
    is_passing=is_passing_grade,
    normalize_grade=normalize_grade
  )
@

Let's verify that the report agrees with the summary on surveys, and that
the optional patterns reach the survey report.
<<test functions>>=
class TestMissingSurveys:
    """Test that --missing judges surveys by completion."""

    <<missing survey test methods>>
<<missing survey test methods>>=
def test_completed_survey_with_lowered_grade_not_missing(
        self, grading_objects):
    user = grading_objects.user()
    survey = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(
        "50", submitted_at="2026-01-11T10:00:00Z"))

    assert list(missing_assignments([survey], [user])) == []

def test_uncompleted_survey_missing(self, grading_objects):
    user = grading_objects.user()
    survey = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(None))

    rows = list(missing_assignments([survey], [user]))

    assert rows == [(user, survey, "mandatory survey not completed")]

def test_optional_survey_not_missing(self, grading_objects):
    survey = grading_objects.survey(None, name="Optional survey")

    rows = list(missing_assignments([survey], [grading_objects.user()],
                                    optional_assignments=["Optional"]))

    assert rows == []
@

Both the summary and the missing-assignment output should collapse [[must fix]]
to the ordinary failing grade token.
<<test functions>>=
class TestMustFixGrade:
    """Test normalization of the Canvas [[must fix]] grade."""

    def test_summarize_treats_must_fix_as_f(self):
        """A [[must fix]] grade should fail participation grading."""
        user = SimpleNamespace(id=1)
        assignment = SimpleNamespace(name="Seminar")
        assignment.get_submission = Mock(
            return_value=SimpleNamespace(
                grade="must fix",
                submitted_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z",
                graded_at=None,
                submission_history=[],
            )
        )

        grade, grade_date, graders = summarize(user, [assignment])

        assert grade == "F"
        assert grade_date is not None
        assert graders == []

    def test_missing_assignments_reports_normalized_f(self):
        """Missing output should collapse [[must fix]] to [[F]]."""
        user = SimpleNamespace(id=1)
        assignment = SimpleNamespace(name="Seminar")
        assignment.get_submission = Mock(
            return_value=SimpleNamespace(
                grade="must fix",
                submitted_at=None,
                graded_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z",
            )
        )

        missing = list(missing_assignments([assignment], [user]))

        assert len(missing) == 1
        assert missing[0][0] is user
        assert missing[0][1] is assignment
        assert missing[0][2] == "not a passing grade (F)"
@
